 {:seon.task/name "every-seon-proc-follows-one-flow-standard-with-a-runnable-example-each"
  :seon.task/plan "Goal: one standard every Seon proc follows, written once in docs/flow.md (stays at most 110 lines, shorter), each pattern with a small runnable example that is a test, so it stays true; plus an inventory of what should be a Flow proc and is not, each filed as a task for its owner. Root approved 2026-11-13; context indexes the examples so an agent touching a pattern is shown them. What exists: docs/flow.md (110 lines), web-research/flow-source.md (upstream against Jolt, line by line), deps' Flow gaps A-E, and port names in use (:to-api x7, :in x5, :main x4, fifteen one-offs). Standard: (1) naming: in-ports named for what arrives, out-ports :to-<pid> when feeding one proc else named for what leaves, replies on the asker's :answers, one key for a reply address and :seon.bound/bound for a message's bound; (2) lifecycle: init takes nothing from outside but in-ports/out-ports, pause and resume idempotent, stop releases everything and answers every open request :stopped, a transition's throw is reported with its command, pause leaves in-flight messages in buffers [M]; (3) patterns each with an example: request/reply by rid, completion as close, fan-out, fan-in, back-pressure, pipelines (lift1->step), deadlines as messages, effects; (4) not allowed: atom as proc state, thread outside declared edges, blocking take in a step, poll, clock read, FIFO-position answers. Measured at the REPL on Jolt, n at most 4 [M]: rid replies match; a throwing step does not stop the proc; an output to a missing coord gives no error, only ::flow/undeliverable on the report chan; a closed in-port yields one nil in order; a deadline via a/timeout onto the own in-port arrives as a message; an open request at stop is not answered by Flow, it is the owner's job. Examples: test/seon/flow_patterns_test.clj, one deftest per pattern, 15-25 lines, ~8 tests ~5M ticks, red first under a mutation. Inventory tasks: the test child's link (second EDN codec, a guard turning undecodable into a value, a pid stamped in the decoder) is built first after the standard: one codec jfasl, an unreadable value as the link's typed error, fan-in knowing its senders; the agent's state and link atoms (agent.clj:36, 41); await-reply's promise table (agent.clj:235); the 7888 server's accept thread (server.clj:633); tree-runs atom (system.clj:2126); server's index-now atom (server.clj:69). Left alone: fork_server loop, fx read-lines! thread, intr and uncaught atoms (edges), caches (values kept by input). Survey (runtime, the Flow specialist, 2026-11-14; notes runtime-flow-survey-2026-11-14.md) [M]: the standard is one pattern per need: a step-fn Var with schema'd state; a conn for a dependency; seon.step/put-after! on the proc's own in-port for any wait past a bound (never (timeout ms) in a step: docs/flow.md's retry idiom goes); pend/ask/answered with one :seon.step/request-id for a proc's ask; ask-edge for an edge's; seon.fx owner-proc and worker-proc per effect kind; one edge reader thread per blocking read; run-loop for a process that must fork. Found duplicated: three request-id schemes for one pattern (step/ask's :rid and :tag, fx's :dest {:agent :op :k}, the agent's :k from :tx-seq): one key, :seon.step/request-id (the schema has it), and one asker helper; owners of system, prove, render, reconcile and fx review. docs/flow.md's Idioms becomes the standard table, each row with its runnable example (the proof this task names). Design guards (the owner, 2026-11-14: proofs that prevent bad designs, not only tests of a fix), each a bin/laws law like clocks and measures, refusing a site the primer does not name with its reason: (1) buffers: every sliding-buffer and dropping-buffer in src is named in docs/flow.md#channel-idioms with the one stream whose newest value subsumes the rest (found 2026-11-14: a sliding buffer carrying many branches' notices was agreed before review caught it); (2) threads: every Thread., future, a/thread and a/io-thread in src is named as an edge reader for a blocking read or a :mixed worker (found: seon.repl/serve-item!'s two io-threads per item, the exit watch's io-thread per child); (3) unbounded waits: every deref of a promise or blocking take with no bound in src is named as an edge whose producer always answers (found: fx!'s await-reply with no bound, the model call's hidden stall). The primer is one page read whole (docs/flow.md, core-async merged in 7de4e6d47e), and a running graph takes new code and procs with no restart (seon.step-test/a-running-graph-takes-new-code-and-new-procs-with-no-restart). Refinement (the owner, 2026-11-14: \"it doesn't seem like the design is that refined. Why do we have three io only procs?\"; plan in notes runtime-flow-refinement-plan-2026-11-14.md, against upstream's \"communication-free functions\"): (1) the agent's three procs exist because steps block ([M] repl.clj:1303-1475: :repl runs a whole work! in one step waiting on promises :transport delivers; :tests runs a whole test run in one step): a turn becomes a step (the model call sent with fx-send!, its outcome a message, the next step runs the reply's evals), evals stay synchronous with their declared bound, :tests goes (a test run and an item each a test-run-step proc added by update!), leaving :transport (the socket's router) and :repl; deletes tests-step, serve-item!'s io-threads, work!'s in-step loop and :draining. (2) Flow's hazards at source in our Jolt Flow: control put with offer! and close outside the graph lock (no wedge), no default clocks (compute, ping, stop declared by the caller; finish-outputs folded into stop's one deadline), the report chan piped into root's :back as problems (Seon reads it nowhere today [M], so undeliverable outputs are lost past 100); casts unused [M], explained. (3) docs/flow.md keeps four bounded warnings (an eval inside its step, a mult's slowest tap, a single-stream sliding drop, a changed proc's restart); the rest become impossible by fix or law. Order 90/10: report pipe