 {:seon.task/name "every-request-is-answered-at-each-end-of-its-holder"
  :seon.task/plan "flow, 2026-11-15, root's order: spine 1 of notes/flow-collapse-plan-2026-11-15.md (one way to ask and be answered). What exists [M, git grep at jolt/flow]: seon.step/pend, ask and answered are the asker's side, used by the reconciler, render, prove, fx, vector and root's api (14 asks). The answerer's side has no one mechanism. Root's api keeps a rid table (seon.system/pend); the agent proc keeps :inflight, released by any of its ends since d8d209489c; an agent keeps :waits for await-reply (9 calls); seon.system/await-db keeps preds; the reconciler keeps :waits and :judged; ask-edge puts a promise and leaves its end to the proc. Every hang this week was an entry one of those tables released only on success (:holding, :judged, a peer's read, clone!'s await-reply). The design (90/10, no new mechanism): (1) the answerer's contract, docs/flow.md#the-five-rules rule 3: a proc that holds a request owes exactly one answer on every path; (2) seon.step/end-all, (st, why) -> [st' answers]: every entry the proc holds answered with the typed :seon.step/ended {:why} (a stop, its in-port's EOF, a step's defect, a declared bound) and its table emptied; each holder calls it at each of its ends: its ::flow/stop transition, the EOF of the port its requests came on, its step-error edge; (3) the holders converted onto it, one area a landing: the agent's await-reply (:waits -> the transport proc's pending, answered by end-all at root's EOF or stop), root's api rid table and agent-proc's :inflight (their release-by-any-end code becomes the one call), the reconciler's :waits/:judged (its stop transition calls end-all). The law :unended-wait (web-2, flow's definition) finds the remaining sites. Estimate: a pending entry is an assoc, O(1) ~1 us; end-all is O(pending) once an end; ~30 lines plus ~3 a holder converted; src net negative. Deletes: each holder's own end-answering code (agent-proc's end-hold paths, the reconciler's stop doseqs, await-reply's finally) -> end-all; :waits -> pend. No functionality removed: every request is still answered, now on every path. Proofs, red first: seon.step-test/every-pending-request-is-answered-at-each-end (a proc holding 3 requests answers all 3 :seon.step/ended at its stop, at its in-port's EOF and at a step's throw; red today: the throw path answers none); seon.agent-test/an-await-reply-ends-when-root-goes (an eval waiting on root's answer is answered :seon.step/ended when root's end closes; red today: it waits until the process ends).\nINSTANCES, 2026-11-15 [M]: (a) data: root's agent proc step threw on an agent's hello (agent-frame building a refused contract error from :load-errors), its state was kept and the frame dropped, so the spawn waiting on that hello was never answered: spawn! waited 887 s, until root stopped (agent c4e03df303b3b556, :last-frame-ms nil, result/a567040a; the throw result/c8810c0e). A step that throws must answer what waits on its input (the spawn with the error) or end what it holds (end-all on the step's own failure), never drop it. (b) flow: seon.server/serve-connection!'s @(c/effect! sys :server-op ...) has no end when the owner loses the answer (task seon.server/an-eval-at-root-always-answers-its-asker). One rule covers both: every wait held by a proc ends at its owner's answer, the owner's typed refusal, the owner's failure, or root's stop.\nAPPROVED (root, 2026-11-15, gap 1; plan notes flow-every-request-answered-plan-2026-11-15.md): a defect in an agent's proc is that agent's end, by the one path a refused spawn takes. In the api's :error arm (system.clj:921-945), when the pid is one of :agent-procs, call spawn-failed (system.clj:374): it records the error on the agent, answers its spawn, and sends it {:op :failed}, so it hangs up and its :ended runs end-agent, which answers every in-flight request. Nothing new; the :reconciler-defect special case stays (the reconciler isn't an agent). Proof red first: seon.problems-test/a-defect-on-an-agents-frame-ends-the-a